    topic: "Learning to swim as an adult",
    passage: "Dilnoza Karimova had been afraid of deep water since she was seven years old, when she fell into a river near her grandmother's village in Fergana. For almost thirty years she had avoided swimming pools, beaches and even boat trips. Then, in January, her doctor told her that her back problems would improve if she found a gentle form of exercise. He suggested swimming, and Dilnoza, who was thirty-four at the time, felt her stomach turn. However, she decided that if she did not face her fear now, she never would.\n\nShe found an adult beginners' class at a pool in Tashkent that ran on Tuesday and Thursday evenings at half past six. The teacher, a patient man named Rustam, had taught more than two hundred adults to swim. On the first evening, he did not ask anyone to get into the water at all. Instead, the group sat on the edge of the pool and put their feet in. Rustam explained that most adults are not afraid of water itself but of losing control, and that the first skill to learn is simply to breathe calmly.\n\nThe next three weeks were difficult. Dilnoza practised blowing bubbles, floating on her back with a foam board and kicking while holding the wall. Twice she nearly gave up, and once she cried in the changing room because the other students seemed to be progressing faster. Rustam told her that if she had compared herself with the others, she would have felt worse, and that she should only compare herself with the person she had been last week. This advice helped her more than any technique.\n\nIn the sixth week something changed. Rustam asked her to push off from the wall and glide, and she travelled almost four metres without touching the bottom. She was so surprised that she swallowed some water and coughed for a minute, but she was also smiling. By the end of March she could swim a full length of twenty-five metres, and by June she was swimming thirty minutes without stopping. Her back pain had almost disappeared.\n\nToday Dilnoza swims three mornings a week before work. She says the biggest lesson was not about swimming at all. It was the discovery that a fear which has lasted for decades can be reduced step by step. Next summer she plans to swim in a lake with her nephew, who is nine and has been teasing her about being a slow learner.",
