    topic: "A healthy breakfast around the world",
    passage: "Breakfast is often described as the most important meal of the day, but what people actually eat in the morning varies enormously from country to country. A recent study by a group of nutritionists, led by Professor Kamola Ergasheva, compared the breakfasts of families in six countries and tried to discover which habits were really good for health. The results, which were published in a university magazine, were more complicated than many people had expected.\n\nIn Japan, the typical breakfast includes rice, grilled fish, a bowl of soup and pickled vegetables. It contains little sugar and a lot of protein, and the researchers found that Japanese children who ate it regularly concentrated better at school. In Turkey, a table is often covered with olives, cheese, tomatoes, cucumbers, eggs and fresh bread. Families sit together for at least thirty minutes, and Professor Ergasheva believes that the social part of the meal is almost as valuable as the food.\n\nThe situation in other places was less encouraging. In the United Kingdom and the United States, many children start the day with sweet cereal, which can contain as much sugar as a chocolate bar. In some parts of Central Asia, a quick breakfast of tea with white bread and jam is common, because mornings are busy and people say that they have no time to cook. The researchers do not criticise these families but note that the meal gives energy for only an hour or two.\n\nProfessor Ergasheva's team also tested the claim that skipping breakfast always makes people gain weight. They found that the answer depends on the person. Some adults who skipped breakfast ate more at lunch and dinner, while others managed perfectly well. The only group that clearly suffered from missing the morning meal was children under twelve, who were less attentive and more irritable before midday.\n\nThe researchers conclude with three suggestions. First, include some protein, such as eggs, yoghurt or beans. Second, choose whole grains instead of white flour. Third, eat with other people when it is possible. According to the professor, none of these changes is expensive, and all of them can be introduced slowly, one morning at a time.",
