    topic: "A mountain hiking weekend",
    passage: "Last June, a group of eight friends from Tashkent decided to spend a weekend hiking in the Chimgan mountains. The idea came from Akmal, a geography teacher who had been to the area many times and promised that the views were worth the effort. Most of the group were beginners, and the only woman with serious experience was Laylo, who had once climbed a peak of four thousand metres. Everyone agreed to meet at the bus station at six on Saturday morning.\n\nThe preparation had started a week earlier. Laylo sent the group a list of things to bring, and she was strict about it. Each person needed comfortable boots, a warm jacket, a waterproof coat, two litres of water, a hat and sunscreen. She advised them not to carry heavy bags, because every extra kilogram feels like five after several hours of climbing. Dilshod, the youngest member, ignored her advice and packed a large tin of fruit, which became a joke for the rest of the trip.\n\nThe first day's route was about twelve kilometres long and climbed from fifteen hundred to twenty-three hundred metres. The weather was sunny, and the group stopped three times for rest. Around noon, they reached a small waterfall and ate lunch beside it. In the afternoon, however, Dilshod's boot began to hurt his heel, and Akmal showed him how to use a plaster and change his socks. They arrived at their campsite at five o'clock, tired but happy, and cooked soup on a small gas stove.\n\nThat night, the temperature dropped to four degrees, and several people could not sleep because of the cold. Nobody complained, though, because the sky was full of stars brighter than any of them had ever seen in the city. In the morning, they climbed for another hour to a rocky ridge and enjoyed a view over the snowy peaks while they drank hot tea.\n\nThe journey home was quiet, as most people fell asleep on the bus. When they arrived back in Tashkent, Laylo asked whether anyone wanted to repeat the trip in the autumn. Eight hands went up immediately, and Dilshod promised to bring a bigger tin.",
