    topic: "Drinking more water",
    passage: "Many people know that they should drink more water, but few of them actually do it. Feruza Abdullaeva, a school librarian in Karshi, was one of those people. For years she drank two cups of tea in the morning, a coffee in the afternoon and almost nothing else. She often had headaches around three o'clock and felt sleepy during the last lessons. It never occurred to her that the reason might be as simple as a lack of water, until a visiting nurse gave a talk at her school.\n\nThe nurse explained that the human body is about sixty percent water and that even mild dehydration can cause tiredness, headaches and difficulty concentrating. She said that most adults need about two litres of fluid a day, though the exact amount depends on the weather, physical activity and body size. A useful sign, she added, is the colour of urine. If it is pale yellow, the person is probably drinking enough, but if it is dark, it is time to have a glass of water.\n\nFeruza decided to run a personal experiment for one month. She bought a one-litre bottle and a set of rubber bands. Her plan was to fill the bottle twice a day and drink it all before dinner. At first she forgot constantly, so she set an alarm on her phone for ten o'clock, noon, three o'clock and five o'clock. She also made the water more attractive by adding slices of lemon or cucumber, since she thought that plain water was boring.\n\nThe results arrived faster than she had expected. After five days, her afternoon headaches had disappeared. After two weeks, a colleague commented that her skin looked healthier. By the end of the month, she had also reduced her coffee to one cup a day, which she had not planned at all. She felt less hungry between meals, which she explained by the fact that thirst is sometimes confused with hunger.\n\nFeruza now shares her bottle trick with her pupils. She keeps a poster in the library with the question, Have you had any water today, and she says that the children are more willing to listen than the adults. Her only complaint is that she now visits the bathroom more often than before.",
