    topic: "A grandfather's walking habit",
    passage: "At eighty-one, Karim Nazarov walks six kilometres every morning, and his neighbours in Margilan set their watches by him. He leaves his house at exactly five forty-five, wearing the same grey cap and carrying a wooden stick which he rarely uses. His grandson, Temur, who is a journalist, became curious about this habit and decided to spend a week walking with his grandfather in order to write an article about it.\n\nOn the first morning, Temur expected to find a slow old man and was surprised by the speed. They walked past the bazaar, along the river and through a small park where Karim greeted every person by name. Karim explained that he began to walk after he retired from the silk factory at sixty, because he did not know what to do with his time. His wife had been ill then, and the doctor had advised him to find a way to reduce stress. A short walk became a long walk, and the long walk became a habit that he had kept for twenty-one years.\n\nDuring the week, Temur discovered that the walk was about much more than exercise. It was also a social event. Karim stopped to talk to the baker, who gave him a warm piece of bread, to a group of old men who played chess on a bench, and to a young mother who asked for advice about her sick child. He seemed to know everybody, and everybody seemed glad to see him. Temur realised that his grandfather had built a community around the habit.\n\nKarim has a few simple rules. He never takes his phone, because he says that it spoils the silence. He drinks a glass of water before leaving and eats a handful of walnuts after returning. He walks in all weathers except heavy rain, and in winter he wears two pairs of socks. He also believes that a person should never walk alone for too long, which is why he often invites neighbours to join him.\n\nWhen the article was published, it was shared thousands of times online. Temur received messages from readers in several countries, and some of them asked whether his grandfather would be willing to lead a walking club. Karim, when he heard about it, laughed and said that he was too busy walking.",
