    topic: "Staying healthy during exam season",
    passage: "Every year in May and June, university students in Uzbekistan face the same problem. Exams arrive, nights become shorter and healthy habits are the first thing to disappear. Dr Zarina Qodirova, who works at the student health centre at the National University, has watched this pattern for fifteen years. She says that the students who perform best in exams are not necessarily those who study the longest, but those who look after their bodies while they study.\n\nAccording to Dr Qodirova, the most common mistake is to sacrifice sleep. Students believe that an extra three hours of study at night will help them remember more facts, but research shows the opposite. During sleep, the brain organises what it has learned, and without it, information is quickly forgotten. She recommends at least seven hours a night, and she advises students never to study in the last hour before bed, because the mind needs time to relax.\n\nThe second mistake is poor food. Students living in dormitories often survive on energy drinks, biscuits and instant noodles, which give a sudden burst of energy followed by tiredness. Dr Qodirova suggests simple alternatives, such as bananas, nuts, boiled eggs and brown bread, which release energy slowly. She also recommends keeping a bottle of water on the desk, because even slight dehydration reduces concentration.\n\nPhysical activity is the third element. Many students stay at their desks for ten hours without moving. Dr Qodirova suggests a ten-minute walk every two hours and a longer walk or some light sport once a day. This does not take time away from studying, she says, because it improves blood flow to the brain and reduces anxiety. At her centre, she also runs a free breathing class every Tuesday at four, which more than sixty students attended last year.\n\nFinally, she reminds students that it is acceptable to ask for help. Stress before exams is normal, but if a student cannot sleep for several nights, feels panic or loses appetite completely, it is time to talk to a doctor or a counsellor. Dr Qodirova ends her advice with a sentence that she often repeats. An exam measures what you know today, not who you are.",
